    <ChildContent>
        <DocSection Id="demo" Title="Live demo"
                    Description="Ask about the weather: the model calls get_weather, waits for the result, and answers from it.">
            <DemoExample Title="A tool call and its answer" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example1CodeFiles" Id="example1">
                <AiScenarioFrame>
                    <BitAiChat Session="_session"
                               EmptyTitle="Ask about the weather"
                               EmptyBody="The model will call get_weather, wait for the result, and answer from it."
                               Suggestions="_suggestions" />
                </AiScenarioFrame>
            </DemoExample>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="how-it-works" Title="How it works">
            <p>
                The transport does not run the tool. On the first request it shows a status line while the model decides
                (<code>BitAiStatusEvent</code>, which the call then replaces), asks for the tool with
                <code>BitAiToolCallStartedEvent</code>, streams its arguments as <code>BitAiToolCallDeltaEvent</code>s, closes
                them with <code>BitAiToolCallCompletedEvent</code> and finishes with <code>BitAiFinishReason.ToolCalls</code>.
                The session then resolves the tool from its registry, checks the approval policy, runs it, records the result
                on the call (<code>BitAiToolCallPart.Result</code>, a <code>BitAiToolOutcome</code>), and asks the model
                again. The transport reads the result off the message it is handed on that second request and answers from
                it, which is what lets an app decide where tools run without the transport knowing. The reply stays one
                message, busy from the first request to the last.
            </p>
            <p>
                Arguments stream in as raw JSON and are parsed leniently on every delta, so an open card fills in key by key
                before the closing brace arrives rather than waiting for valid JSON. The cards here start collapsed
                (<code>BitAiUiOptions.CollapseToolCalls</code>) and these arguments arrive in a fifth of a second; it shows
                with a model writing a long argument, such as the content of a file.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="declaring" Title="Declaring a tool">
            <p>
                A tool is an <code>IBitAiTool</code>: a <code>Definition</code> and an
                <code>InvokeAsync(BitAiToolInvocation, CancellationToken)</code> that returns a <code>BitAiToolOutcome</code>.
                The <code>BitAiToolDefinition</code> is what the model is told and what the card shows: the <code>Name</code>
                the model calls, a <code>Description</code> it reads when deciding to, the <code>ParametersSchema</code> of
                its arguments (a JSON Schema), and for the UI a <code>Title</code>, an <code>Icon</code>, its
                <code>Risk</code> and whether it <code>RequiresApproval</code>
                (<BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/approval">Tool approval</BitLink>). One instance serves every conversation, so
                a tool keeps no state of its own. A failure is best returned as <code>BitAiToolOutcome.Failure(...)</code>;
                a tool that throws anyway is logged, and its call gets a failed result whose message is the exception's,
                retryable, with the exception kept on the error.
            </p>
            <p>
                <code>BitAiTool.Create</code> builds one from a delegate, where it is registered, deriving the schema from
                the argument type:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>Create&lt;TArgs, TResult&gt;(name, description, argumentsTypeInfo, resultTypeInfo, (args, token) =&gt; ..., configure)</code> - the handler takes the arguments and returns a result, written back to the model as JSON. This page's <code>get_weather</code>.</li>
                <li><code>Create&lt;TArgs&gt;(name, description, argumentsTypeInfo, (args, invocation, token) =&gt; ..., configure)</code> - the handler is also handed the <code>BitAiToolInvocation</code> and returns the whole <code>BitAiToolOutcome</code>: its display <code>Text</code>, its <code>Citations</code>, a structured failure (<BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/custom-tool">Custom tool UI</BitLink>'s <code>search_docs</code>).</li>
                <li>The same two without the <code>JsonTypeInfo</code>s read the types by reflection, so they are marked as unsafe to trim.</li>
                <li><code>Create(definition, (invocation, token) =&gt; ...)</code> - nothing derived: the schema is whatever the definition holds, for arguments that are dynamic.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The <code>JsonTypeInfo</code> overloads take the metadata of the app's own <code>JsonSerializerContext</code>,
                so the schema is derived and the arguments are read without reflection, and a trimmed WebAssembly app - like
                this site - keeps working. <code>configure</code> adjusts the generated definition
                (<code>definition =&gt; definition with { Title = ..., Risk = ... }</code>).
                <code>BitAiTool.CreateSchema</code> derives a schema on its own (<code>CreateSchema(typeInfo)</code>, safe to
                trim, or <code>CreateSchema&lt;T&gt;()</code> by reflection) for a definition written by hand.
            </p>
            <p>
                A tool that needs services is a class of its own, registered with <code>ai.AddTool&lt;TTool&gt;()</code>, as
                the <b>WeatherTool.cs</b> tab shows. Each call hands it a <code>BitAiToolInvocation</code>: the
                <code>ToolCallId</code> and <code>ToolName</code>, the <code>Arguments</code> as JSON
                (<code>GetArguments(typeInfo)</code> reads them into the argument type, <code>GetArguments&lt;T&gt;()</code> by
                reflection), the <code>ConversationId</code> and <code>MessageId</code> it belongs to, the scoped
                <code>Services</code> to resolve dependencies from, <code>Progress</code> to report how far it has got (never
                null), and the app's own <code>Context</code> values.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="registry" Title="The registry and the request">
            <p>
                The registered tools live in an <code>IBitAiToolRegistry</code> - a <code>BitAiToolRegistry</code>, a
                case-insensitive map from a tool's name to the tool, one for the app (its members are listed on
                <BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolcallview#tool-registry">BitAiToolCallView</BitLink>). It can change at run
                time (<code>Register(tool)</code>, <code>Unregister(name)</code>), which is how an app offers a capability
                only to some users or conversations; a session reads it on every request. This page builds its session by
                hand, over a transport of its own, and hands it the registry
                (<code>new BitAiChatSession(transport, tools: registry)</code>); a session from
                <code>BitAiChatSessionFactory</code> gets the registry, the approval policy, the configured session options
                and the scoped services its tools resolve from without being asked - a session built by hand gets services
                only through its <code>services</code> argument.
            </p>
            <p>
                Every request carries the definitions of those tools (<code>BitAiRequest.Tools</code>, what
                <code>BitAiToolRegistry.GetDefinitions()</code> returns), so a transport hands
                the model what it may call without knowing how the tools were registered, and the session's
                <code>RequestOptions</code> (<code>BitAiRequestOptions</code>) say how it may call them:
                <code>ToolChoice</code> is <code>BitAiToolChoice.Auto</code> (the model decides),
                <code>None</code>, <code>Required</code> (some tool), or <code>Specific("get_weather")</code> (that one). A
                transport maps the choice onto its provider's API, and is free to ignore what it cannot express.
            </p>
            <p>
                A tool the provider runs itself - a web search on its side - is reported by the transport, not run by the
                session: <code>BitAiToolCallStartedEvent</code> with the complete <code>Arguments</code>, any number of
                <code>BitAiToolProgressEvent</code>s while it runs (a <code>BitAiToolProgress</code> with a
                <code>Fraction</code> and a <code>Message</code>, drawn as the card's progress bar), then
                <code>BitAiToolResultEvent</code> with its outcome. The session never runs a call that already has a result,
                so local and provider tools can mix in one reply; a transport whose tools all run on its side turns
                <code>BitAiChatSessionOptions.ExecuteTools</code> off. The <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/agent">Multi-step agent</BitLink>
                scenario's search is one of these. The <b>Request options</b> tab shows a tool choice, and a provider tool
                reported with its progress.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="components" Title="Components used">
            <ul>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolcallview"><code>BitAiToolCallView</code></BitLink> - the card: what is called, its arguments as they stream, its progress and its result.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/jsonview"><code>BitAiJsonView</code></BitLink> - the arguments and the result inside it.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/thinkingindicator"><code>BitAiThinkingIndicator</code></BitLink> - the status line shown while the model decides.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/chat"><code>BitAiChat</code></BitLink> - the chat over the session that runs the tool.</li>
            </ul>
        </DocSection>
